<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
<Fragment set:html={"<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"shell\"><div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/resources/\">Resource Center</a> / <a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Options &amp; Upgrades</a> / 04</div><div class=\"eyebrow\">CUSTOMIZATION TOPIC 04 \u00b7 Structure &amp; Exterior</div><h1>Construction Materials &amp; Structural Standards</h1><p>The standard framing specification is the starting point, not an upgrade.</p></div><section class=\"asi-cost-review\" id=\"option-planning-prices\"><h2>Option pricing &amp; budget planning</h2><p class=\"asi-cost-alert\"><strong>Research-stage planning references:</strong> The figures below come from historical ASI planning material or approved model siding formulas. Historical amounts have NOT been verified as current Appalachian Sheds selling prices. They are not quotes. Final prices and any credits for included standard features must appear in a written proposal.</p><table class=\"asi-cost-list\"><thead><tr><th>Option / scope</th><th>Planning figure</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nonstandard PT tongue-and-groove structural floor</td><td>$375\u2013$1,075</td><td>common sizes, legacy reference; not standard specification</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"asi-cost-note\">Standard ASI building features remain included in the approved model price. Figures shown are for planning context only; these are not added to the online shed calculator. No site-specific price should be inferred from a price per linear or square foot.</p><a href=\"/options-gallery/\">Explore visual option comparisons</a></section></section><div class=\"shell deep-layout\"><article><section class=\"deep-answer\" id=\"overview\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">THE ESSENTIAL ANSWER</div><h2>What should you know before choosing?</h2><p>A pressure-treated beam can still be wrong if it is not rated for the actual exposure. A structural panel can still be wrong if its span rating does not match the framing. Premium siding can still fail if the backing, water management, clearances or fasteners are incorrect. Material quality and installation quality have to work together.</p><p>Ground-contact beams and other wood exposed to severe moisture belong in the correct preservative-treatment use category. AWPA identifies UC4A as the general-use ground-contact category.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-1\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 01</div><h2>Compare the Assembly \u2014 Not Just the Material.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A pressure-treated beam can still be wrong if it is not rated for the actual exposure. A structural panel can still be wrong if its span rating does not match the framing. Premium siding can still fail if the backing, water management, clearances or fasteners are incorrect. Material quality and installation quality have to work together.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Foundation &amp; Ground Contact</h3><p>Ground-contact beams and other wood exposed to severe moisture belong in the correct preservative-treatment use category. AWPA identifies UC4A as the general-use ground-contact category.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Floor Framing &amp; Decking</h3><p>Joist size, spacing, beam support and structural floor selection work as one system. ASI floor specifications remain model- and size-specific rather than one universal framing rule.</p><p>Stud dimension and spacing should match the model, wall height, openings, exterior system and intended interior finish. A larger stud is not automatically a better wall if the complete design is not coordinated.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior Siding System</h3><p>Siding, backing, WRB / flashing, trim, fasteners, clearances and finish requirements should be specified together. The visible siding is only the outer part of the wall assembly.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-2\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 02</div><h2>Roof Deck &amp; Roofing</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Roof sheathing thickness and span rating must match rafter / truss spacing and load requirements. Roofing, underlayment, drip edge and ventilation then complete the weather system.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Fasteners &amp; Connectors</h3><p>Pressure-treated wood can be more corrosive to steel. Fasteners and connectors should be selected for the preservative chemistry and exposure rather than treated as interchangeable hardware.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Finish &amp; Maintenance</h3><p>Paint, stain, caulk and exposed-edge protection must match the substrate. Finish requirements are part of the material specification, not decoration added at the end.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Both Can Be Structural Panels \u2014 But They Are Not Automatically Interchangeable</h3><p>APA recognizes both plywood and OSB as structural wood panels when they carry the appropriate performance rating. The correct choice depends on application, exposure, span rating, stiffness, fastener performance, jobsite moisture and the written specification.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-3\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 03</div><h2>\u201cExposure 1\u201d Does Not Mean \u201cExterior.\u201d</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Exposure 1 panels can tolerate construction moisture but are not intended for permanent weather exposure. Exterior bond-classification plywood is designed for repeated wetting and drying or long-term weather exposure.</p><p>The panel stamp and end use matter more than generic labels such as \u201cplywood,\u201d \u201cOSB,\u201d or \u201cCDX.\u201d</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>\u201cPressure Treated\u201d Is Not a Complete Specification</h3><p>Preservative-treated lumber is produced for different exposure conditions. Ground-contact members should carry treatment appropriate for ground contact rather than an above-ground rating selected only because it is less expensive.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Look for UC4A or the Appropriate Higher Use Category.</h3><p>The treatment tag should identify the use category, preservative and retention information needed to match the lumber to its exposure.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>The Correct Lumber Still Needs the Correct Metal</h3><p>Preservative chemicals can accelerate connector and fastener corrosion. Simpson Strong-Tie and the treated-wood industry recommend corrosion-resistant fasteners appropriate to the treatment chemistry and exposure.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-4\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 04</div><h2>Match Fastener + Connector + Treatment + Exposure.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Do not mix metals casually and do not assume electro-galvanized hardware is equivalent to hot-dip galvanized or stainless systems.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Five Siding Systems \u2014 Each With Its Own Backing and Preparation Requirements</h3><p>The earlier material guide contained a broader ten-siding catalog. The current Appalachian Sheds program has intentionally been reduced to five exterior systems so the specifications can remain disciplined and easy for customers to compare.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Do Not Add Redundant Sheathing Behind Structural Panel Siding.</h3><p>DuraTemp is itself a plywood siding panel. LP panel products can also serve specific structural / siding roles when installed to their evaluated assembly. Full separate plywood backing is reserved for assemblies where that backing is appropriate.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-5\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 05</div><h2>Price the Difference in the Complete Assembly \u2014 Not Just the Board or Panel</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A legitimate material upgrade price should include the material difference, waste, added fasteners / accessories, labor difference, freight / handling, overhead and the credit for the standard material being replaced.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>No Double-Charging for the Standard Material.</h3><p>When an upgrade replaces an included standard component, the standard component should be credited in the upgrade calculation before the final selling price is established.</p><p>This category establishes material standards and decision logic. Detailed prices remain in the dedicated option category so the same upgrade is not priced two different ways on the website.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>A Premium Material Specification Should Be Clear Enough to Verify</h3><p>Vague language such as \u201ctreated lumber,\u201d \u201cwood floor,\u201d \u201cengineered siding,\u201d or \u201cexterior sheathing\u201d leaves too much unanswered. The written scope should identify the actual material role and performance requirement.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Type \u00b7 Thickness \u00b7 Treatment \u00b7 Spacing \u00b7 Exposure \u00b7 Finish</h3><p>Those details make the material choice meaningful.</p><p>What beam size is used? Is it ground-contact rated? How is it supported and anchored?</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-6\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 06</div><h2>Know What Is Inside the Building \u2014 Not Just What You Can See From the Driveway</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Appalachian Sheds specifies the base, floor, framing, structural panels, siding, roof, fasteners and finish as one coordinated construction system. Detailed selections are then documented by model and option in the written proposal.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"asi-scope\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">IMPORTANT PROJECT DISTINCTION</div><h2>How this relates to an Appalachian Sheds project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Built on your property</h3><p>All Appalachian Sheds buildings are constructed on site. Property access, drainage, topography, location restrictions and the selected model can affect planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior shell versus finished interior</h3><p>Our standard building proposal describes a weather-tight exterior shell. Insulation, electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, and interior wall or ceiling finishes are separate work unless expressly listed in the written scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Only the written scope is authoritative</h3><p>Model-specific specifications, pricing, selected options, finishing requirements and any engineering or permit obligations must be confirmed for the actual building and site. Avoid treating this general guide as an engineering design or a permit determination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section verified-planning\" id=\"verified-planning\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">CURRENT PLANNING GUIDANCE</div><h2>Document the baseline before proposing an upgrade</h2><p>The traditional baseline specifies pressure-treated floor joists and exterior-rated floor decking, kiln-dried wall studs, engineered traditional trusses and CDX roof decking. Model-specific structural variations may apply.</p><p>Added floor capacity, heavy equipment, loft loads, alternate glazing or a modified roof shape can require additional engineering and pricing. These are not standard upgrades that can be assumed suitable for every building.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"next\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">MAKE THE NEXT DECISION</div><h2>Your next planning step</h2><p>Compare this decision with the relevant model features and options, then request a written proposal identifying standard inclusions, any optional work and property-specific requirements.</p><div class=\"related\"><a href=\"/buildings/\">View Models &amp; Pricing</a><a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Explore Building Guides</a><a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Browse Options &amp; Upgrades</a></div></section></article><aside class=\"deep-toc\"><strong>On this page</strong><a href=\"#verified-planning\">Current planning guidance</a><a href=\"#overview\">Essential answer</a><a href=\"#topic-1\">Compare the Assembly \u2014 Not Just the Material.</a><a href=\"#topic-2\">Roof Deck &amp; Roofing</a><a href=\"#topic-3\">\u201cExposure 1\u201d Does Not Mean \u201cExterior.\u201d</a><a href=\"#topic-4\">Match Fastener + Connector + Treatment + Exposure.</a><a href=\"#topic-5\">Price the Difference in the Complete Assembly \u2014 Not Just the Board or Panel</a><a href=\"#topic-6\">Know What Is Inside the Building \u2014 Not Just What You Can See From the Driveway</a><a href=\"#asi-scope\">How this relates to an ASI project</a><a class=\"btn light\" href=\"/buildings/\">See Your Price</a></aside></div>"} />
</Base>

